>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Procurement · Specification Systems · Last reviewed 2026-09-22</p>
      <h1>JIS vs DIN vs BCI: A Buyer's Guide to Battery Specification Systems</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The same lead-acid battery has three names: a JIS code in Tokyo, a DIN code in Munich and a BCI group
        number in Chicago. Each system bundles <strong>size, terminals and ratings</strong> in its own notation — and each
        reads the other two as a trap.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> <strong>BCI</strong> (North America) names batteries by group number (24, 34, 31…);
        <strong>DIN/EN</strong> (Europe) by size codes (H6/LN3…) with EN 50342 ratings; <strong>JIS</strong> (Japan/Asia) by type
        codes (55D23…) with JIS D5301 ratings. The systems are not interchangeable — codes convert approximately
        by dimension, but fitment must be confirmed physically, and ratings must be compared only within their
        own standard.
      </div>

      <h2>The three systems at a glance</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>System</th>
            <th>Region</th>
            <th>Size notation</th>
            <th>Rating standard</th>
            <th>Terminal style</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>BCI</strong> (Battery Council International)</td>
            <td>North America</td>
            <td>Group numbers (24, 27, 31, 34, 34R…)</td>
            <td><a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/sae-j537/">SAE J537</a> (CCA), BCI RC method</td>
            <td>SAE automotive posts, studs, side terminals</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>DIN / EN</strong></td>
            <td>Europe</td>
            <td>Size codes (H5/LN2, H6/LN3, H7/LN4, H8/LN5…)</td>
            <td><a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/en-50342/">EN 50342</a>, <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/din-43539/">DIN 43539</a></td>
            <td>European taper posts (19/17.9 mm), recessed</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>JIS</strong></td>
            <td>Japan / Asia</td>
            <td>Type codes (55D23, 80D26, 105D31…)</td>
            <td><a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/jis-d5301/">JIS D5301</a></td>
            <td>Pencil posts, often recessed, R/L polarity</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>How each system encodes size</h2>
      <h3>BCI group numbers</h3>
      <p>
        A BCI group number is a published recipe of case dimensions, terminal type, terminal position and
        polarity. The suffix R or L fixes polarity (positive-right or positive-left). The number itself carries
        no rating information — two <a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/battery-groups/group31/">Group 31</a> batteries can differ
        widely in CCA and Ah, which is exactly why the group alone is never a complete specification. See
        <a href="/lead-acid/battery-group-sizes/">battery group sizes</a> for the system in detail.
      </p>
      <h3>DIN/EN size codes</h3>
      <p>
        European practice uses trade size codes — H6/LN3, H7/LN4 and so on — mapped to case dimensions and the
        standard European recessed-terminal geometry. The "H" family (H5–H8) covers the common mid-size car
        battery classes; see <a href="/lead-acid/h6-vs-h7-vs-h8/">H6 vs H7 vs H8</a> for how the codes step in size. Ratings
        travel under EN 50342, with its own CCA method and Ah at the 20-hour rate.
      </p>
      <h3>JIS type codes</h3>
      <p>
        A JIS type code such as 55D23 is a compact sentence: the leading number is a performance rank (a
        composite of capacity and cranking class), the letter is a width-and-height class, and the trailing
        digits give the case length in centimetres. The system is dense and internally consistent, but the
        performance rank is <em>not</em> a CCA figure — it is a category. Buyers who read "55" as amps are
        misreading the code. The data sub-site's <a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/battery-groups/">battery group reference</a>
        covers how the codes map to physical packages.
      </p>

      <h2>The cross-system traps</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The CCA trap.</strong> JIS D5301 tests at −15°C; EN 50342 at −18°C with a 7.5V cut-off; DIN at −18°C with
          a 9.0V cut-off. The same physical battery prints a higher number under JIS. Comparing a JIS CCA to an
          EN CCA is comparing two different tests — see <a href="/lead-acid/cca/what-is-cca/">what CCA is</a> for the full ladder.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The terminal trap.</strong> JIS pencil posts and European taper posts are physically different. A battery
          that fits the tray but not the vehicle's cable ends is a fitment failure with extra steps.
          <a href="/lead-acid/battery-terminal-types/">Terminal types</a> must be specified explicitly, never assumed from a size code.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The conversion trap.</strong> "JIS 55D23 equals BCI 23F-ish" cross-references exist, but they are
          approximate. Treat a converted code as a search hint, then confirm by the dimensioned drawing and
          polarity — never order on a converted code alone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The lookalike trap.</strong> Within one system, nearby codes can be genuinely different batteries (a
          34 vs 34R differs in polarity; a JIS 55B24 vs 55D23 differs in terminal type). The suffix letters are
          load-bearing.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Writing a specification that survives all three systems</h2>
      <p>
        The robust procurement specification does not rely on any single notation. It states, explicitly and
        independently:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Dimensions</strong> — length × width × height in mm, with tolerances.</li>
        <li><strong>Terminal type, position and polarity</strong> — post style, handedness, and the cable end it must accept.</li>
        <li><strong>Ratings with standards</strong> — CCA <em>with its test standard</em>, Ah <em>with its discharge rate</em>, RC with its method.</li>
        <li><strong>Chemistry</strong> — flooded, <a href="/lead-acid/efb-battery/">EFB</a>, <a href="/lead-acid/agm-battery/">AGM</a> or gel.</li>
        <li><strong>Reference codes</strong> — the relevant BCI group, EN size code or JIS type code as a cross-check, not as the specification itself.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Written this way, the requirement survives translation between systems and between markets — the same
        reason the <a href="/procurement/how-to-read-battery-datasheet/">datasheet reading guide</a> insists every figure carry its
        condition.
      </p>

      <h2>What procurement buyers should check</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Is the specification stated in dimensions, terminals and ratings — not only in one system's code?</li>
        <li>Are CCA figures compared only within the same test standard (JIS to JIS, EN to EN, SAE to SAE)?</li>
        <li>Are terminal type and polarity stated explicitly, including the R/L or handedness suffix where it applies?</li>
        <li>Is any cross-system code conversion confirmed against a dimensioned drawing before ordering?</li>
        <li>Does the quotation restate the spec in the supplier's local system without silently changing a figure?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What are JIS, DIN/EN and BCI battery specification systems?</h3>
      <p>They are three regional systems for describing lead-acid starter batteries. JIS (Japan) uses codes like 55D23; DIN/EN (Europe) uses size codes like H6/LN3 plus EN 50342 ratings; BCI (North America) uses group numbers like 24, 31 or 34R. Each system bundles case dimensions, terminal style and ratings in its own notation.</p>
      <h3>Can you convert a JIS battery code to a BCI group number?</h3>
      <p>Approximately, via dimension and terminal cross-reference tables, but not exactly — fitment should be confirmed by physical dimensions, terminal type and polarity, not by a converted code. Cross-reference tables are a starting point, not a purchasing guarantee.</p>
      <h3>Why do JIS CCA numbers look higher than DIN/EN numbers?</h3>
      <p>Because the tests differ. JIS D5301 tests at -15°C while EN 50342 tests at -18°C with a 7.5V cut-off and DIN at -18°C with a 9.0V cut-off. The warmer JIS test prints a higher number for the same physical battery. Comparing JIS to EN CCA without conversion is the classic cross-system trap.</p>
      <h3>Which system should a procurement specification use?</h3>
      <p>The system of the destination market and the vehicles it serves — BCI for North America, DIN/EN for Europe, JIS for Japan/Asia. The safest practice is to specify dimensions, terminal type, polarity and the CCA standard explicitly, so the requirement survives any notation system.</p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>
        Compiled from the three standards bodies' published definitions and battery industry fitment practice;
        cross-system conversions are described as approximate because the systems define sizes independently.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Battery Council International (BCI) — group number definitions (dimensions, terminals, polarity).</li>
        <li>EN 50342 — European lead-acid starter battery standard (sizes, ratings, test conditions).</li>
        <li>JIS D5301 — Japanese automotive lead-acid battery standard (type codes, ratings, test conditions).</li>
        <li>DIN 43539 — German starter battery test method (−18°C, 30 s, 9.0V).</li>
        <li>SAE J537 — North American cranking-ampere test method (−18°C, 30 s, 7.2V).</li>
        <li>World Battery Hub data sub-site — <a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/battery-groups/">battery group reference</a> for cross-system size data.</li>
        <li>World Battery Hub testing sub-site — standard pages for <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/sae-j537/">SAE J537</a>, <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/en-50342/">EN 50342</a>, <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/jis-d5301/">JIS D5301</a>, <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/din-43539/">DIN 43539</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-22</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
